# The capability ladder

### Seven rungs from a seed to the real thing

Tutors Ladder, Orientation, talk 2

---

## The idea

> "The course would invite students to rebuild the Tutors from the very simplest version, through to the
> most sophisticated. Each rung in the ladder requires some additional expertise to understand, but
> delivers major new capabilities."

From the project lead's proposal for this course.

---

## The rungs

| Rung | Variant | Capability delivered |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `rung-1-seed` | Course folder in, `tutors.json` out |
| 2 | `rung-2-lite` | A static HTML website from `tutors.json` |
| 3 | `rung-3-layers` | The same tools as a layered pnpm workspace with ports |
| 4 | `rung-4-reader` | An interactive Svelte 5 reader |
| 5 | `rung-5-proven` | Tests as specification |
| 6 | `rung-6-guarded` | Safe content, private courses, a secure supply chain |
| 7 | `rung-7-shipped` | A versioned, gated, containerised release |
| Summit | tutors-mono-repo | The real thing |

---

## The expertise each rung adds

| Rung | New expertise |
| --- | --- |
| 1 Seed | TypeScript on Node, the file system, the course format, discriminated unions |
| 2 Lite | Markdown rendering, HTML templates, escaping, exhaustive rendering |
| 3 Layers | Workspaces, dependency direction, ports and adapters, architecture tests |
| 4 Reader | Svelte 5 runes, SvelteKit routing, components, Vite |
| 5 Proven | EARS, Given/When/Then, honest doubles, golden files, mutation testing |
| 6 Guarded | XSS and sanitising, path containment, identity ports, RBAC, CSP, supply chain |
| 7 Shipped | CI, SemVer, changelogs, containers, release claims, SLOs |

---

## How each rung maps to upstream

| Rung | Mirrors in tutors-mono-repo |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | `packages/jsr/gen`, `packages/jsr/model`, `packages/jsr/tutors` |
| 2 | `packages/jsr/tutors-lite` |
| 3 | `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `packages/jsr/types`, `course-rules` ports, `tests/architecture` |
| 4 | `apps/reader`, `packages/svelte/ui-*` |
| 5 | `tests/`, `guides/testing`, `stryker.config.json` |
| 6 | `packages/jsr/identity`, `packages/svelte/utils/rbac`, `docs/security`, `SECURITY.md` |
| 7 | `.github/workflows`, `release/`, `Dockerfile`, tutors-release-harness |

Each variant's `RUNG.md` names the exact files.

---

## The shape of the ladder

```
              Summit: tutors-mono-repo
       /        |         |         \
   4 Reader  5 Proven  6 Guarded  7 Shipped
       \        |         |         /
              3 Layers
                 |
              2 Lite
                 |
              1 Seed
```

Rungs 1 to 3 are one line. Rungs 4 to 7 each start from a copy of rung 3, so take them in any order.

---

## Why rebuild from the simplest version?

- **You can read it whole.** Rung 1 fits in an afternoon. The mono-repo does not fit in a week.
- **Every line has a reason you have met.** Nothing arrives before the rung that explains it.
- **You experiment on real features.** Each rung is a working Tutors, so you can break it and watch.
- **The names match upstream.** `Course`, `Topic`, `Lab`, `LabStep`, `"talk"`, `"web"`: what you learn
  carries straight across.

---

## Why these tracks?

- **CLI** and **UI (static)**: the generator and the simplest reader, the two halves of Tutors
- **Architecture**: the mono-repo is a layered workspace; you need that map before the big pieces
- **UI (app)**, **Testing**, **Security**, **Releasing**: four independent skills, each a large part of
  the real repository

---

## What a variant holds

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `README.md` | run it in three commands |
| `RUNG.md` | the Rung Delta: capability, expertise, upstream mirror table |
| `AGENTS.md` | the short manifest for a coding agent, under 500 words |
| `package.json` | its `test` script is the Single Gate |
| `tests/rung-fence.test.ts` | the Rung Fence |

All under `tutors/variants/` in the AI-Reference-Projects repository.

---

## Folders now, tags later

- This course keeps one folder per rung, because one repository holds the whole ladder
- When the ladder moves to its own repository, each rung becomes a tag: `rung-1-seed` to `rung-7-shipped`
- Rungs 4 to 7 become branches from `rung-3-layers` until they are composed
- Composing them is the Summit's capstone assignment

---

## How to climb

1. Read the topic's talks
2. Read the variant's `RUNG.md`
3. Run its gate: `npm test` for rungs 1 and 2, `pnpm test` from rung 3
4. Do the lab: change the variant and keep the gate green
5. Do the assignment
6. Open the upstream files the rung mirrors and compare

Next: how the Assertion Ladder keeps each rung safe.
